{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-82350",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079520/assets/0119869a8627edfaf9c16f905908d378b94c9566b0a6b7742c813c35f58d4699.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2x-2\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type II regions. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079520/assets/3fe0d83ae8f99f443646be4e84687104edaeef58a83e68da7e832630f31efda7.png)\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y),$  is \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\frac{y+2}{2}.$\n\nAlso, the right boundary of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\sqrt[3]{1-y}.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{y+2}{2}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{1-y}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x-2}^{1-{x}^{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{(y+2)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{1-y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
